1
00:00:00,270 --> 00:00:01,960
They call it a demo.

2
00:00:01,990 --> 00:00:03,470
It is a business.

3
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:08,560
This API bills per call, holds a treasury, and has customers who pay.

4
00:00:08,590 --> 00:00:10,436
Nobody is at the keyboard.

5
00:00:10,766 --> 00:00:13,056
Software you buy can be copied.

6
00:00:13,086 --> 00:00:15,063
Its customers cannot.

7
00:00:15,093 --> 00:00:19,395
An agent marketplace needs traffic, and a repo ships none.

8
00:00:19,665 --> 00:00:22,687
So we sell the business, not the build.

9
00:00:22,717 --> 00:00:27,176
Every token is an NFT that owns one running paid API.

10
00:00:27,206 --> 00:00:30,071
Hold the token, and the revenue is yours.

11
00:00:30,341 --> 00:00:33,519
Live on testnet, read straight from the contract.

12
00:00:33,549 --> 00:00:36,728
Nine tokens, every figure verifiable.

13
00:00:36,758 --> 00:00:41,869
Open one: owner, worker key, vault, gas, earnings.

14
00:00:42,139 --> 00:00:44,587
The sale is one transaction.

15
00:00:44,617 --> 00:00:47,664
Vault, key and customers move together.

16
00:00:47,694 --> 00:00:51,160
Forty of forty obligations. None dropped.

17
00:00:51,450 --> 00:00:53,584
We attacked our own protocol.

18
00:00:53,614 --> 00:00:56,349
Six attacks. Zero succeeded.

19
00:00:56,379 --> 00:01:00,680
Our own customers faking demand? The appraisal says skip.

20
00:01:00,970 --> 00:01:07,597
The run: sixty-six paid calls, zero point six six revenue, zero point five four net.

21
00:01:07,627 --> 00:01:10,492
Four point four days to earn back the price.

22
00:01:10,522 --> 00:01:14,746
The strict policy still says no. The numbers are the numbers.

23
00:01:15,116 --> 00:01:16,204
Outlive.

24
00:01:16,234 --> 00:01:19,100
A business that outlives the person who started it.
